{
  "image_name": "36b5e84cde17cccc05429aaddfc6650f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) interact with particles in four environments—soil, marine and lake sediment, river sediment, and marine snow—and highlights the different functions they support in each setting. Across all panels, EPS appears as a sticky matrix coating mineral, clay, sand, and detrital particles and connecting them with microbes and other small components in water.\n\nThe common theme is that EPS binds particles together, changes pore spaces, and alters how material moves through sediments or aggregates. In soil, EPS bridges particles, promotes aggregation, traps enzymes and debris, contributes to carbon storage, and can clog or block pores. In marine and lake sediments, it surrounds grains and microbial cells, helping nutrient acquisition, carbon storage, sediment stabilization, and sometimes sediment mobility. In river sediment, the diagram emphasizes flow, showing EPS-coated grains forming more stable structures that resist erosion but may also reduce hydraulic conductivity and cause clogging. In marine snow, EPS links diverse suspended particles and microorganisms into loose sinking aggregates, affecting aggregation, settling, and the transport of carbon to the seafloor. Overall, the figure presents EPS as a key biological glue controlling sediment structure and material cycling.",
